From 758f6e0faa3b23b14bc7227ae2a3a8d3740808f4 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: dev <dev@local>
Date: Sun, 18 Oct 2026 14:47:24 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 24/29] feat(netease): answer the artist page, which was 404
 for its whole lifetime
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Opening an artist from search showed an empty page - and on the wall layout that
means one poster filling the screen, so the artist's name was blown up over the
seam while every label under it said "(nothing)". The cause was not layout: the
local implementation never had the three routes that page is built from, so
`/artist/detail`, `/artist/album` and `/artist/top/song` all fell through to the
"no such route" answer of `{code:404}`, and the page dutifully rendered nothing.

All three are now translated from the reference implementation
(@neteasecloudmusicapienhanced/api, module/artist_detail.js, artist_album.js,
artist_top_song.js):

- `/artist/detail` → `/api/artist/head/info/get` over eapi, which is what the
  reference's default crypto resolves to;
- `/artist/album` → `/api/artist/albums/{id}` over weapi with limit, offset and
  total. The id is in the PATH here, not the payload, so the route table's `uri`
  now accepts a function as well as a string;
- `/artist/top/song` → `/api/artist/top/song` over weapi.

Responses pass through untouched, because the reference passes them through too:
`netease.ts` reads `data.artist`, then `hotAlbums`/`more`, then `songs`/`privileges`.

Verified against the live API rather than assumed - test/manual/netease_artist_probe.ts
replays the shell's own proxy envelope from Node and hits music.163.com with this
transport, and all three answer 200 with those shapes (probe output recorded in the
file's header). The unit tests pin the two things that are easy to get wrong and
invisible when wrong: the artist id reaching the URL path, and the payload surviving
the trip unchanged.
